# API Development Guidelines

> **Note:** This document provides comprehensive instructions for AI agents working on the Spring Boot API layer. Always refer to `/​.ai/​overall.mdc` for project-wide standards and conventions.

## Architecture & Design Patterns

### Layered Architecture

Follow strict separation of concerns:

* **Controller Layer (`@RestController`)**: Handle HTTP requests/responses, input validation, and response formatting
* **Service Layer (`@Service`)**: Implement business logic, transaction management, and coordinate between components
* **Repository Layer (`@Repository`)**: Data access logic using Spring Data JPA
* **Entity Layer**: Domain models mapped to database tables
* **DTO/ViewModel Layer**: Request/Response data transfer objects

**Rule**: Never access repositories directly from controllers. Always go through service layer.

### Dependency Injection

* Use **constructor injection** (preferred) with `@RequiredArgsConstructor` from Lombok
* Avoid field injection (`@Autowired` on fields)
* Keep dependencies immutable where possible

```java
@Service
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class UserService {
    private final UserRepository userRepository;
    private final PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder;
}
```

## REST API Conventions

### HTTP Methods

* `GET`: Retrieve resources (idempotent, cacheable)
* `POST`: Create new resources
* `PUT`: Replace entire resource (rarely used)
* `PATCH`: Update resource (preferred for updates) **and soft deletes**
* **Important**: This project uses `PATCH` for soft deletes, **NOT** `DELETE`

### Response Format

All API responses must follow this standard structure:

```java
public class ApiResponse<T> {
    private boolean isSuccess;
    private String message;
    private T data;
    private LocalDateTime timestamp;
}
```

### HTTP Status Codes

* `200 OK`: Successful GET, PATCH
* `201 Created`: Successful POST (resource created)
* `400 Bad Request`: Validation errors, malformed requests
* `401 Unauthorized`: Missing or invalid authentication
* `403 Forbidden`: Valid auth but insufficient permissions
* `404 Not Found`: Resource doesn't exist
* `409 Conflict`: Duplicate resource or business rule violation
* `500 Internal Server Error`: Unhandled server errors

### Endpoint Naming

* Use plural nouns: `/api/v1/users`, `/api/v1/cards`
* Use path parameters for IDs: `/api/v1/users/{userId}`
* Use query parameters for filtering: `/api/v1/users?status=active`
* Keep URLs lowercase with hyphens: `/api/v1/flash-cards`

## Coding Style & Standards

### Method Naming Conventions

* **Controllers**: `getUsers()`, `createUser()`, `updateUser()`, `patchUser()`
* **Services**: `findUserById()`, `createNewUser()`, `updateUserDetails()`, `deleteUserSoftly()`
* **Repositories**: Standard Spring Data JPA names or custom `findBy...()` methods

### Java Best Practices

* Use Lombok annotations: `@Data`, `@Builder`, `@Slf4j`, `@RequiredArgsConstructor`
* Prefer immutability: Use `final` for variables that won't change
* Use meaningful names: `userId` not `id`, `userEmail` not `email`
* Avoid magic numbers: Define constants for fixed values
* Add JavaDoc for public methods in service layer

```java
/**
 * Creates a new user with the provided details.
 *
 * @param createUserRequest The user creation request containing email and name
 * @return ApiResponse containing the created user data
 * @throws UserAlreadyExistsException if email is already registered
 */
public ApiResponse<UserViewModel> createNewUser(CreateUserRequest createUserRequest) {
    // Implementation
}
```

### Validation

* Use Jakarta Bean Validation annotations: `@Valid`, `@NotNull`, `@Email`, `@Size`
* Validate at controller layer for input DTOs
* Implement custom validators for complex business rules
* Return detailed validation errors with field-level messages

```java
@PostMapping("/users")
public ApiResponse<UserViewModel> createUser(@Valid @RequestBody CreateUserRequest request) {
    return userService.createNewUser(request);
}
```

### Error Handling

* Use `@ControllerAdvice` for global exception handling
* Create custom exceptions extending `RuntimeException`
* Map exceptions to appropriate HTTP status codes
* Include helpful error messages (but never expose sensitive data)

```java
@ControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {
    @ExceptionHandler(UserNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<Void>> handleUserNotFound(UserNotFoundException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
            .body(ApiResponse.error(ex.getMessage()));
    }
}
```

### Logging

* Use `@Slf4j` from Lombok
* Log at appropriate levels:
  * `DEBUG`: Detailed flow information
  * `INFO`: Important business events
  * `WARN`: Recoverable issues
  * `ERROR`: Failures requiring attention
* **Never log sensitive data**: passwords, tokens, full credit card numbers
* Structure logs for easy parsing: Include request IDs, user IDs, timestamps

```java
@Slf4j
@Service
public class AuthService {
    public ApiResponse<TokenResponse> login(LoginRequest request) {
        log.debug("Login attempt for email: {}", request.getEmail());
        // ... business logic
        log.info("User {} logged in successfully", userId);
    }
}
```

## Database & JPA

### Entity Design

* Use `@Entity` with meaningful table names via `@Table(name = "users")`
* Use UUID for primary keys: `@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.UUID)`
* Implement soft deletes with `isDeleted` boolean field
* Add audit fields: `createdAt`, `updatedAt`, `createdBy`, `updatedBy`
* Use appropriate relationships: `@OneToMany`, `@ManyToOne`, `@ManyToMany`

```java
@Entity
@Table(name = "users")
@Data
public class User {
    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.UUID)
    private UUID id;
    
    @Column(nullable = false, unique = true)
    private String email;
    
    private boolean isDeleted = false;
    
    @CreatedDate
    private LocalDateTime createdAt;
    
    @LastModifiedDate
    private LocalDateTime updatedAt;
}
```

### Repository Pattern

* Extend `JpaRepository<Entity, ID>` or `CrudRepository<Entity, ID>`
* Use Spring Data query methods: `findByEmail()`, `existsByEmail()`
* Use `@Query` for complex queries with JPQL or native SQL
* Add `@Modifying` for UPDATE/DELETE queries

```java
@Repository
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, UUID> {
    Optional<User> findByEmailAndIsDeletedFalse(String email);
    boolean existsByEmailAndIsDeletedFalse(String email);
    
    @Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.isDeleted = false")
    List<User> findAllActiveUsers();
}
```

## Security

### Authentication & Authorization

* Use Spring Security with JWT tokens
* Implement `JwtAuthenticationFilter` for token validation
* Use `@PreAuthorize` for method-level security
* Store passwords with BCrypt: `passwordEncoder.encode(password)`

```java
@PreAuthorize("hasRole('ADMIN')")
@PatchMapping("/users/{userId}/role")
public ApiResponse<UserViewModel> updateUserRole(...) {
    // Only admins can access this
}
```

### Security Best Practices

* Never return passwords in API responses
* Validate JWT tokens on every request
* Implement rate limiting for authentication endpoints
* Use HTTPS in production
* Sanitize all user inputs to prevent injection attacks

## Testing Requirements

### Unit Tests (JUnit + Mockito)

* **All test methods must have `@DisplayName` annotations** with clear, descriptive names
* Achieve ≥90% code coverage
* Test method naming: `methodName_whenCondition_shouldExpectedOutcome`
* Mock all external dependencies with `@Mock` or `@MockBean`
* Use `@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)` for JUnit 5

```java
@ExtendWith(MockitoExtension.class)
class UserServiceTest {
    
    @Mock
    private UserRepository userRepository;
    
    @InjectMocks
    private UserService userService;
    
    @Test
    @DisplayName("Should create user successfully when email is unique")
    void createUser_whenEmailIsUnique_shouldCreateSuccessfully() {
        // Arrange
        CreateUserRequest request = new CreateUserRequest("test@example.com", "Test User");
        when(userRepository.existsByEmail(anyString())).thenReturn(false);
        
        // Act
        ApiResponse<UserViewModel> response = userService.createNewUser(request);
        
        // Assert
        assertTrue(response.isSuccess());
        verify(userRepository).save(any(User.class));
    }
}
```

### Test Coverage Requirements

* **Controllers**: Test request validation, response formatting, status codes
* **Services**: Test business logic, edge cases, error handling
* **Repositories**: Test custom queries (standard Spring Data methods don't need tests)
* **Security**: Test authentication, authorization, JWT validation

### Test Data

* Use builders or factories for creating test objects
* Avoid magic values: Use constants or descriptive variables
* Test edge cases: null values, empty strings, boundary conditions
* Test security: unauthorized access, missing permissions

## API Documentation

### SpringDoc OpenAPI

* Add `@Tag` to controller classes
* Add `@Operation` to endpoints with descriptions
* Add `@ApiResponses` to document all possible responses
* Add `@Parameter` for path/query parameters
* Add `@Schema` to DTOs for field descriptions

```java
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/v1/users")
@Tag(name = "User Management", description = "APIs for managing users")
public class UserController {
    
    @Operation(summary = "Create new user", description = "Creates a new user with the provided details")
    @ApiResponses(value = {
        @ApiResponse(responseCode = "201", description = "User created successfully"),
        @ApiResponse(responseCode = "400", description = "Invalid input"),
        @ApiResponse(responseCode = "409", description = "Email already exists")
    })
    @PostMapping
    public ApiResponse<UserViewModel> createUser(@Valid @RequestBody CreateUserRequest request) {
        return userService.createNewUser(request);
    }
}
```

## Performance Considerations

* Use pagination for list endpoints: `@PageableDefault`
* Add database indexes for frequently queried columns
* Use `@Transactional` appropriately (read-only for queries)
* Implement caching for expensive operations with `@Cacheable`
* Use lazy loading for entity relationships when appropriate

## Common Patterns

### Soft Delete Pattern

```java
@PatchMapping("/{id}")
public ApiResponse<Void> deleteUser(@PathVariable UUID id) {
    User user = userRepository.findById(id)
        .orElseThrow(() -> new UserNotFoundException("User not found"));
    user.setDeleted(true);
    user.setDeletedAt(LocalDateTime.now());
    userRepository.save(user);
    return ApiResponse.success("User deleted successfully");
}
```

### Pagination Pattern

```java
@GetMapping
public ApiResponse<Page<UserViewModel>> getUsers(@PageableDefault(size = 20) Pageable pageable) {
    Page<User> users = userRepository.findAllByIsDeletedFalse(pageable);
    Page<UserViewModel> viewModels = users.map(UserViewModel::from);
    return ApiResponse.success(viewModels);
}
```

